          Key Findings from the 2026 State Rate Audit
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>A $2,470 State-Line Gap:</strong> The spread between the cheapest state (<strong>Maine at $1,170/yr</strong>) and the priciest (<strong>Florida at $3,640/yr</strong>) now tops <strong>$2,470 a year</strong> for the exact same clean-record driver.</li>
          <li><strong>The No-Fault Premium:</strong> States requiring Personal Injury Protection (PIP)—like Florida, Michigan, and New York—routinely land near the top of the cost list because of heavier medical billing and fraud exposure.</li>
          <li><strong>Paying for Uninsured Drivers:</strong> Where more than 20% of motorists drive without coverage (including Mississippi, Michigan, and Tennessee), insured drivers absorb roughly $320 extra a year to cover uninsured motorist claims.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

