      <h2>Why Do These Specific States Rank at the Extremes?</h2>

      <h3>The 3 Most Expensive States: Florida, Michigan & Louisiana</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Florida ($3,640/yr avg):</strong> Florida drivers get hit from four directions at once: a 20.4% uninsured motorist rate, mandatory PIP coverage, frequent hurricanes and severe storms, and heavy third-party litigation against insurers.</li>
        <li><strong>Michigan ($3,480/yr avg):</strong> Even after recent no-fault reforms, Michigan carries steep costs tied to its history of unlimited lifetime medical benefits and mandatory Michigan Catastrophic Claims Association (MCCA) assessments on every insured vehicle.</li>
        <li><strong>Louisiana ($3,320/yr avg):</strong> Louisiana's "direct action" law lets injured parties sue an insurance company directly rather than the driver, fueling higher lawsuit rates and steeper bodily injury payouts.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The 3 Cheapest States: Maine, Vermont & Idaho</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maine ($1,170/yr avg):</strong> Under 4.5% of Maine drivers are uninsured, traffic is light outside a few small cities, auto theft is rare, and traditional fault rules keep claim payouts predictable.</li>
        <li><strong>Vermont ($1,210/yr avg):</strong> Mostly rural roads, fewer severe multi-car pileups, and low lawsuit volume keep claim costs modest for carriers operating in the state.</li>
        <li><strong>Idaho ($1,265/yr avg):</strong> Less congested metro areas, open highways, and strict enforcement against uninsured drivers help hold base rates down.</li>
      </ul>

